{"t":1791657290,"office":"LWX","station":"KDCA","point":"38.8894,-77.0352","field":"38.9429,-76.5684","field_station":"KNAK","afd":{"id":"aa4ae8f2-b125-4f11-9c2f-21099c2de0f7","office":"LWX","issuanceTime":"2026-10-10T14:07:00+00:00","productText":"\n000\nFXUS61 KLWX 101407\nAFDLWX\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Baltimore MD/Washington DC\n1007 AM EDT Sat Oct 10 2026\n\n.WHAT HAS CHANGED...\nThe leading edge of some light rainfall is ahead of schedule as\nit nears I-64 this morning. Otherwise, continue to monitor the\n12Z high-resolution guidance for expected rainfall on Sunday as\nwell as degree of severe weather/tornado threat.\n\n&&\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n- 1) Remnants of Hurricane Isaias to bring rain, breezy to \n  windy conditions, and the threat of tornadoes Sunday.\n\n- 2) Above normal temperatures Tuesday will fall the second \n  half of the week as moisture starved fronts move through.\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Remnants of Hurricane Isaias to bring rain, breezy \nto windy conditions, and the threat of tornadoes Sunday.\n\nAlthough the remaining circulation from Isaias is still down\nacross Georgia, the extent of this system extends well to the\nnorth. While a longstanding high cirrus shield remains overhead,\nsome weak to modest radar returns are evident south of I-64.\nWhile there is still low-level dry air to contend with, expect\nthe column to saturate in time. Otherwise, the forecast remains\non track (read below for more information).\n\nHurricane Isaias is forecast to move north toward the TN Valley \ntoday as it weakens and eventually loses all of its tropical \ncharacteristics. The mid-level circulation strengthens and becomes a \nlarge cutoff low later tonight. To the east, surface high pressure \nover the Northeast today moves offshore off southern New England \ntonight. \n\nThe interaction of Isaias' remnants to the west and surface high to \nthe east will create a large area of convergence / broad frontal \nzone over the Mid-Atlantic. There is good model agreement that rain \nsteadily moves from south to north this evening into tonight as \nforcing for ascent increases with some mid-level height falls in \nresponse to the approaching cutoff low. It'll take a bit of time for \nthe surface to truly saturate, so any rain late afternoon to this \nevening in Central Virginia is going to be light. Eventually, \ntropical moisture with PWATs at or above 2\" surges in after \nmidnight. \n\nIt is worth mentioning again that conditions are favorable for a \nwidespread soaking rain, and 00Z models have responded with more QPF \nacross the region. Forcing for ascent increases tonight, and \nespecially Sunday morning into Sunday afternoon as mid-level heights \nfall with the approach of the cutoff low. Enhanced low-level \nconvergence and moisture transport will peak Sunday morning into \nearly Sunday afternoon. Strengthening easterly flow into the Blue \nRidge could also produce enhanced rain amounts due to orographic \neffects. As a result, forecast rain amounts have increased to 1.5-\n2.5\", with locally higher amounts of 3-4\" possible. No Flood Watch \nissued with this forecast update as the heaviest rain is forecast to \noccur in areas that can handle it, but will continue to monitor the \nlatest guidance today.\n\nAnother noticeable trend in the 00Z guidance is the higher \nprobabilities for severe weather Sunday afternoon. The main \nsynoptically forced rain moves north of the area early Sunday \nafternoon. Rapid height-falls and increasing low-level winds provide \na few hour window where some breaks in the clouds area possible, \nleading to pockets of instability. A surface wave of low pressure \nmoves right through our area Sunday afternoon. Forecast soundings \nindicate large, swooping hodographs, with ample shear in place  to \nsupport organized severe convection. Clusters of thunderstorms \ndevelop along the leading edge of the surface low pressure, with \ntornadoes and damaging wind gusts as the main threats (looks mostly \nto be east of the Blue Ridge). The NAM12, RRFS, and 06Z HRRR all \nshow this scenario. The big question still remains how much \ninstability actually develops. SPC has introduced a Slight Risk for \nSunday just to our south, with a Marginal Risk north to the MD/PA \nborder.\n\nThe strong mid-level circulation is going to bring windy conditions \nto the Alleghenies and Blue Ridge, starting late tonight into Sunday \nmorning. Wind Advisories have been issued for downsloping easterly \nwinds in the Alleghenies (western Garrett, western Grant, and \nwestern Pendleton Counties), and the Blue Ridge. The current \nforecast for peak wind gusts is 40-50 mph in the Alleghenies/Blue \nRidge and 20-30 mph elsewhere.\n\nThe remnants of Isaias push offshore Monday, bringing an end to any \nlingering showers. While conditions return humid and warm Monday, \nthere likely won't be much in the way of shower activity due to \nsubsidence behind the departing trough.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Above normal temperatures Tuesday will fall the \nsecond half of the week as moisture starved fronts move through.\n\nBrief ridging in the wake of the tropical remnants may bring high \ntemperatures Tuesday to near 80 degrees. Low pressure passing north \nof the area will send a cold front southward Tuesday night. Most of \nthe rain likely remains across Pennsylvania, although some showers \nmay sneak southward. Showers will also be possible along the \nAllegheny Front. There's still some significant model spread on the \nprogression of this front during the middle of the week, with a \nlarge spread of temperatures possible on Wednesday. However, the \ngeneral trend will be downward for the second half of the week. A \nwave of low pressure or cold front may approach toward the end of \nthe week which could bring some rain, but model spread remains high \non the details.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION /14Z SATURDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nVFR conditions prevail through this evening, then conditions \ndeteriorate tonight into Sunday as the remnants of Hurricane Isaias \nimpact the region. IFR conditions are likely to overspread all \nterminals late tonight through Sunday afternoon, as periods of rain \nand gusty winds cross the region. There could be a few hour period \nof thunderstorms Sunday afternoon, some of which could be severe, \nthough this threat is greatest east of the Blue Ridge. Conditions \nimprove Monday morning, with the return of VFR conditions. Northwest \nwinds gusting around 20-25 knots possible Monday afternoon.\n\nVFR conditions are most likely Tuesday and Wednesday. A dry cold \nfrontal passage Tuesday night may be followed by gusty northwest \nwinds Wednesday.\n\n&&\n\n.MARINE...\nMarine conditions are expected to deteriorate tonight through Sunday \nnight as the remnants of Hurricane Isaias move across the Mid-\nAtlantic. SCA conditions develop this evening in the central \nChesapeake Bay, then across all the waters tonight. The strongest \nwinds, gusts around 30 knots, will occur Sunday morning. Special \nMarine Warnings may be needed Sunday afternoon for thunderstorms \ncapable of producing strong wind gusts and waterspouts. The remnants \nof Isaias move offshore Monday morning, but SCA conditions continue \nthrough Monday evening due to gusty northwest winds in its wake.\n\nSub-SCA conditions are likely Tuesday with brief high pressure. SCA \nconditions are possible in southerly flow Tuesday night and \nnorthwesterly flow on Wednesday as a cold front pushes through.\n\n&&\n\n.TIDES/COASTAL FLOODING...\nWidespread tidal flooding is likely during the Sunday afternoon to \nevening high tide cycle. Several conditions are coming together to \nproduce a surge of water up the Chesapeake Bay and Potomac River. \nStrong onshore winds coming during the highest of the daily tides, \nwhich happens to coincide with the New Moon are going to produce \nminor tidal flooding at most forecast locations. A Coastal Flood \nWatch has been issued for Annapolis where Moderate tidal flooding is \npossible. Water levels quickly decrease Monday morning as offshore \nwinds follow.\n\n&&\n\n.LWX WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nDC...None.\nMD...Small Craft Advisory from 1 AM to 11 PM EDT Sunday for MDZ008.\n     Coastal Flood Watch from Sunday afternoon through Sunday \n     evening for MDZ014.\n     Wind Advisory from 8 PM this evening to 11 AM EDT Sunday for \n     MDZ509.\nVA...Wind Advisory from 2 AM to 11 AM EDT Sunday for VAZ507-508.\nWV...Wind Advisory from 8 PM this evening to 11 AM EDT Sunday for \n     WVZ501-505.\nMARINE...Small Craft Advisory from 1 AM to 11 PM EDT Sunday for \n     ANZ530>533-535-536-538>542.\n     Small Craft Advisory from 7 PM this evening to 11 PM EDT \n     Sunday for ANZ534-537-543.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...ADS/BRO/KRR\nAVIATION...ADS/KRR\nMARINE...ADS/KRR\nTIDES/COASTAL FLOODING..KRR\n"},"forecast":{"t":1791657228,"days":{"2026-10-10":{"hi":71,"lo":62,"pop":93,"short":"Cloudy"},"2026-10-11":{"hi":70,"lo":64,"pop":100,"short":"Showers And Thunderstorms"},"2026-10-12":{"hi":72,"lo":59,"pop":33,"short":"Chance Showers And Thunderstorms then Partly Sunny"},"2026-10-13":{"hi":78,"lo":64,"pop":2,"short":"Sunny"},"2026-10-14":{"hi":79,"lo":59,"pop":8,"short":"Sunny"},"2026-10-15":{"hi":70,"lo":57,"pop":22,"short":"Mostly Sunny"},"2026-10-16":{"hi":69,"lo":55,"pop":30,"short":"Chance Rain Showers"}}},"grid":{"t0":1791655200,"n":48,"ceil":[23000,22600,21800,21600,19700,20800,17500,12000,10700,9400,8800,8000,6400,5100,3300,1300,1100,1000,800,700,600,500,400,400,300,300,300,300,300,300,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null],"vis":[10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,9.8,8.1,7.5,5.5,4.1,2.4,1.8,1.8,1.7,1.8,2.3,1.0,1.0,1.0,4.0,4.0,4.0,1.0,1.0,1.0,3.0,5.0,5.0,5.0,5.0,5.0,4.0,4.0,4.0,5.0,5.0,5.0,5.0,5.0,5.0],"spd":[7,8,8,8,8,7,7,7,6,6,6,6,6,7,8,9,10,10,11,11,12,12,13,13,13,12,11,11,